WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.121
FIPS 203 is the Module-Lattice-Based Key-Encapsulation Mechanism Standard.

00:00:03.121 --> 00:00:05.388
The standardized algorithm name is ML-KEM,

00:00:05.388 --> 00:00:09.920
which is associated with the Kyber family from the NIST selection process.

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:13.319
In practical terms, ML-KEM is used for key establishment:

00:00:13.319 --> 00:00:17.851
two parties use public-key operations to agree on shared secret material that

00:00:17.851 --> 00:00:20.495
can then protect communications with symmetric cryptography.

00:00:20.495 --> 00:00:22.761
A key encapsulation mechanism, or KEM,

00:00:22.761 --> 00:00:28.804
is different from the older mental model of encrypting a message directly with a public key.

00:00:28.804 --> 00:00:29.937
In a KEM,

00:00:29.937 --> 00:00:35.602
one party encapsulates a shared secret using the recipient's public key and sends a ciphertext.

00:00:35.602 --> 00:00:38.624
The recipient decapsulates it using the private key.

00:00:38.624 --> 00:00:43.911
Both sides then derive shared key material for protecting the session or protocol exchange.

00:00:43.911 --> 00:00:48.066
ML-KEM is central because many networked systems depend on key establishment.

00:00:48.066 --> 00:00:51.087
TLS, VPNs, service-to-service communication, application protocols, secure messaging,

00:00:51.087 --> 00:00:55.619
and internal platform connections all need a way to establish session keys.

00:00:55.619 --> 00:00:59.396
If the public-key mechanism is vulnerable to future quantum attack,

00:00:59.396 --> 00:01:02.418
long-lived sensitive traffic may be exposed to harvest-now,

00:01:02.418 --> 00:01:05.817
decrypt-later risk depending on the data and threat model.

00:01:05.817 --> 00:01:11.482
Migration will often involve hybrid deployments where classical and post-quantum mechanisms are combined during transition.

00:01:11.482 --> 00:01:14.881
That helps manage compatibility and confidence while ecosystems mature.

00:01:14.881 --> 00:01:19.036
For security teams, the important questions are where key establishment happens,

00:01:19.036 --> 00:01:23.568
which libraries and protocols are in use, which vendors control the cryptography,

00:01:23.568 --> 00:01:28.100
how certificates or keys are managed, and how interoperability will be tested.
